            <ArticleTitle>When in doubt, aspirate.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>648-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/ccd.26484</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Coronary embolization accounts for about 2% of ST elevation myocardial infarctions (STEMI). Thrombus aspiration may be particularly effective in these cases and may prevent the need for coronary stenting. Despite recent guidelines assigning a class III recommendation to routine thrombus aspiration, selective thrombus aspiration based on angiographic appearance may be effective.</AbstractText>
